    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. So it's basically like a safety buffer so no one gets cheated? Who actually has control over the money while it's sitting in there? Like, can the seller just grab it if they want, or is there something preventing that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exactly—it's a safety buffer against fraud or last-minute surprises. While the money is in escrow, **no one** has full control over it: the seller can’t touch it, and you can’t withdraw it without mutual consent. The escrow agent (usually your real estate lawyer or a dedicated escrow company) holds it under the terms of your purchase agreement, and they only release the funds when both parties have fulfilled their obligations (e.g., clean title, financing, inspections). If a dispute arises, the money stays frozen until you both agree on a resolution or a court decides.",
      "source": "target",
